{
  "id": "Compound::DB01567 - Gene::2563",
  "verbalization": "Fludiazepam is shown to interact with Gabrr3, Gabrr2, and Gabrg1, all of which are subunits of Gaba receptors. These receptors are central to the modulation of neuronal activity, and their participation in Gaba Receptor Activity suggests a role in synaptic transmission and neurophysiological regulation. The consistent binding of Fludiazepam to these receptor subunits, coupled with their involvement in Gaba Receptor Activity, implies that Fludiazepam may exert its effects through a mechanism involving the GABAergic system. The shared involvement of these receptors in Gaba Receptor Activity suggests a potential for Fludiazepam to influence GABA-mediated signaling broadly, possibly through a common pathway. The evidence supports a relationship between Fludiazepam and Gabrd, as the receptor subunits are linked to Gaba Receptor Activity, and their participation in this activity is a key component of the mechanism.",
